 ## Background
-The concept of separating focus and context in volume rendering has evolved from simple opacity modulation to more complex structural and geometric integrations [G]. Early work focused on isosurface similarity to indicate stability [S1]. Later, hybrid approaches combined volume rendering with explicit geometry (glyphs, tensorlines) to handle dense tensor fields [8909-hybrid-rendering-for-stress-tensor-fields.md]. Concurrently, structural approaches emerged to handle multiclassified data by modeling logical relationships between classification criteria as directed graphs, enabling boolean queries over structural relationships [14778-structural-focus-context-rendering.md]. The integration of interactive widgets, such as radial cluster trees, allows users to manipulate these structural parameters directly [S2, unofficial].
